# safe-bash-extras.example — copy to safe-bash-extras and edit
#
# This file is COMMITTED in the repo as a template. The live version
# (without `.example`) is gitignored — it's per-machine personal
# config, not project policy.
#
# To use:
#   1. Copy this file:  cp safe-bash-extras.example safe-bash-extras
#   2. Uncomment / add patterns you want auto-allowed on this machine
#   3. On any line you uncomment, delete its trailing `# ...` comment
#      too — the hook matches the WHOLE line, so an inline comment
#      becomes part of the pattern and breaks the match
#   4. Run a quick smoke test in a Claude session — try a matching
#      command and check ~/.claude/hook-audit.log for an ALLOW entry
#
# Each pattern is bash-glob-evaluated against the FULL command string.
# `*` matches any substring including spaces. Patterns are NOT wrapped
# in `Bash(...)` — just the bare pattern as you'd write it informally.

# ─── Format ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One pattern per line. Lines starting with `#` are comments. Blank
# lines are ignored. The hook strips trailing whitespace and trailing
# `\r` (CRLF tolerance) before testing the pattern.
#
# Examples:
#   ls            # exact match: bare command, no args
#   ls *          # the command with any args (note the space + star)
#   git status    # exact "git status" with no args
#   git status *  # "git status" with any args
#
# ─── Why these patterns are safe enough ─────────────────────────────
#
# Every pattern below assumes the hook's Tier 1 (shell composition
# deny) is still active. With Tier 1 ON, none of these can be
# weaponized via `&&`, pipes, `$()`, or redirects — those forms are
# rejected before this file is consulted. If you disable Tier 1 OR
# remove the hook, re-evaluate every entry below against your threat
# model before re-enabling.

# ─── Directory + file creation ──────────────────────────────────────
# Empty directories and empty files are not destructive — they can't
# overwrite existing data, and the dangerous follow-ups (redirecting
# into them, chaining install commands) are blocked at Tier 1.

mkdir *
touch *

# ─── Read-only inspection ───────────────────────────────────────────
# These tools READ filesystem state but don't modify it. Safe to
# auto-allow as long as they're invoked without redirects (which
# Tier 1 catches anyway). Some may be automatic in some cases.

ls *
# pwd
# cat *
# head *
# tail *
# wc *
# file *
# stat *
# tree
# tree *
grep *

# ─── Git read-only operations ───────────────────────────────────────
# These match a subset of what's likely already in the project
# settings.json. Listed here as examples; uncomment only if your
# project's .claude/settings.json doesn't already cover them.

# git status
# git status *
# git log
# git log *
# git diff
# git diff *
# git show *
# git branch
# git branch *

# ─── Niche utilities ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Things you might use occasionally that aren't covered by typical
# project allowlists. Add as you find prompts you're tired of clicking.
# Note that some of these may vary by OS or install.

# figlet *           # ASCII banner generator
# cowsay *           # cow-says-things
# rev *              # reverse each line of input
# jq *               # JSON processor
# yq *               # YAML / structured-data processor
# bash -n *          # Tests syntax, no-op (verify with Bash first!)

# ─── Cleanup reminders ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# - Remove patterns you no longer use; the file is read on every Bash
#   tool call, and noise here is friction during audits.
# - When in doubt, leave a pattern OFF this list. Claude's default
#   prompt-and-confirm flow is the right backstop for anything
#   you're not sure about.
# - The audit log at ~/.claude/hook-audit.log records every match
#   from this file as "extras file: <path>: <pattern>" — useful for
#   spotting drift over time.
